WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
How a cascade works, and how adults really learn.

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.630
This is the video for module one of the Training-of-Trainers course.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:19.780
A normal training teaches a skill people use themselves. A Training-of-Trainers, or ToT, teaches two things at once: the skill, and how to teach that skill to others.

4
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:26.880
Graduates go back to their churches, cooperatives and women's groups and run the same training there.

5
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:40.270
One small team cannot reach every village. But train 20 people properly, and if each trains 25 more, the message reaches 500 people. This chain is called a cascade.

6
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:49.560
The risk is dilution. Like a whispered sentence passed down a line, the message can change at each level unless you protect it.

7
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:56.250
Give every trainer a written guide with the exact key messages, activities and timings.

8
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:07.100
Make every trainer teach at least one real session inside the ToT, and get feedback before she leaves. At least a third of a ToT should be practice teaching.

9
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:14.070
Choose trainers who know the topic, communicate well and have time, not only those with titles.

10
00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.160
Observe or call in on early sessions, and answer questions on a WhatsApp group.

11
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:27.000
And use the same short test and feedback form at every level, so you can see where quality drops.

12
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:41.050
For example, Amaka coordinates a women's network in Anambra. She selects 18 trainers, three in each of six local government areas, who keep their own business records and are trusted in their markets.

13
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:48.620
After a three-day ToT, each trainer agrees to run two sessions of about 15 women within six weeks.

14
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:55.730
That is 18 trainers, times 2 sessions, times 15 women: 540 women.

15
00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:10.610
She plans honestly for drop-out. If only 15 trainers finish, the reach is 450. So her target is 450 to 540, protected by observation visits and shared forms.

16
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:24.050
Malcolm Knowles described how adults learn. They need to know why. They bring years of experience. They want help with real problems, and they see themselves as responsible for their own decisions.

17
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:36.300
So open with the real problem the session solves. Ask for their examples before you teach. Organise content around tasks. And give choices instead of talking down.

18
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:43.420
A market woman who has traded for 15 years already knows a great deal. Connect new tools to what she knows.

19
00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:58.660
David Kolb described learning as a cycle: experience, reflect, draw the lesson, then try it. For example, Halima teaches cooperative members in Kaduna to separate business money from household money.

20
00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:08.380
First, 10 minutes of experience. Two volunteers act out a trader who takes school fees from her sales box, then cannot restock.

21
00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:14.200
Then 10 minutes to reflect in pairs. What went wrong? Has this happened to you?

22
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:21.670
Then 10 minutes for the concept: keep two separate purses, and pay yourself a fixed amount each week.

23
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.840
Finally, 15 minutes to experiment. Each woman writes her weekly amount and tells a partner who will check in.

24
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:40.350
Halima only spoke for about 10 minutes. Many trainers spend all their time explaining, and nothing changes. Use the cycle, and learning sticks.

25
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:53.780
Always ask before taking photos, and offer a clear option to say no. Nigeria's Data Protection Act requires personal data to be collected for a clear purpose and kept secure.

26
00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:02.050
Check extension cables, keep them away from walkways and water, keep exits clear, and do not overcrowd the room.

27
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:11.080
Agree the language, use pictures for women who read little, choose a ground floor room, and plan childcare so mothers can take part.

28
00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:30.210
Run this check the evening before. Venue confirmed. Chairs in circles, not rows, and a backup if the power fails. Kit packed. Forms printed. Access and childcare planned. Co-facilitator briefed. If any box is unticked, fix it or change the plan.

29
00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:49.040
To remember. A ToT teaches the content and how to teach it. Reach is trainers, times sessions, times participants. Guides, practice and follow-up stop dilution. Use all four stages of Kolb's cycle. And plan consent and inclusion before you start.

30
00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:57.540
Now sketch your own cascade with a drop-out figure, redesign one lesson with Kolb's cycle, and make your readiness kit.

31
00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:59.830
Continue with the lessons below.
